   "answer": "Using the Amdahl's Law formula from the lecture, the speedup for a fixed problem size is:\n\nS(p) = 1 / ((1 - f) + f / p) [S1, p.66]\n\n**1. Speedup with 10 processors (p = 10, f = 0.9):**\n\nS(10) = 1 / ((1 - 0.9) + 0.9 / 10)\nS(10) = 1 / (0.1 + 0.09)\nS(10) = 1 / 0.19\nS(10) ≈ 5.26x\n\n**2. Limit with infinitely many processors (p → ∞):**\n\nAs p approaches infinity, the term f/p approaches 0. The formula simplifies to:\n\nS(∞) = 1 / (1 - f)\nS(∞) = 1 / (1 - 0.9)\nS(∞) = 1 / 0.1\nS(∞) = 10x\n\nThis matches the \"NUMERICAL INTUITION\" provided in the lecture, which states that for f = 0.90, the maximum speedup is 10x [S1, p.66]. The serial fraction (1 - f = 0.1) caps the total speedup, meaning no matter how many processors are added, the speedup cannot exceed 10x for this specific parallelizable fraction.",
